> Hospital Emergency Room — Patient Triage (Min-Heap)
    >. In an Indian government hospital ER, patients are not treated on a first-come-first-served basis
      > the most critical cases (lowest severity number = highest priority) get seen first.

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Patient {
    String name;
    int severity;   // 1 = critical, 10 = minor

    Patient(String name, int severity) {
        this.name = name;
        this.severity = severity;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " (severity " + severity + ")";
    }
}

public class EmergencyRoom {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Min-heap: lower severity number = higher priority (critical first)
        PriorityQueue<Patient> erQueue =
                new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.severity, b.severity));

        erQueue.add(new Patient("Ramesh",  3));
        erQueue.add(new Patient("Sunita",  8));
        erQueue.add(new Patient("Vikram",  1));   // heart attack — critical
        erQueue.add(new Patient("Pooja",   5));
        erQueue.add(new Patient("Arun",    2));   // severe accident

        System.out.println("Patients seen in priority order:");
        int count = 1;
        while (!erQueue.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  " + count++ + ". " + erQueue.poll());
        }
    }
}

Patients seen in priority order:
  1. Vikram (severity 1)
  2. Arun (severity 2)
  3. Ramesh (severity 3)
  4. Pooja (severity 5)
  5. Sunita (severity 8)


Why PriorityQueue? In an ER, the most critical patient must be seen first regardless of arrival time.
A LinkedList (FIFO) would treat Sunita before Vikram — fatal. PriorityQueue with a custom comparator solves this in O(log n) per case.
